    "decisionQuestion":"Does a higher-income-yield thesis survive building condition, railway, tenant and flood evidence in north Tokyo?",
    "answer":"A north-Tokyo yield thesis must be station- and building-specific. The 2026 residential averages were JPY 742,000 per m² in Kita, JPY 718,700 in Arakawa, JPY 553,600 in Itabashi and JPY 480,500 in Nerima. Annual changes ranged from 6.2% to 11.8%. Lower land basis than the core can improve income yield, but older stock, river exposure and micro-location exit demand can consume the spread.",
    "priceFact":"Kita JPY 742,000/m²; Arakawa JPY 718,700; Itabashi JPY 553,600; Nerima JPY 480,500","priceMeaning":"The cluster spans a wide basis and should be divided by railway, river and product rather than treated as one affordable market.",
